# Compiled document extension

Identity: `{{.Name}}`. Package: `adapter`. Factory: `{{.Factory}}`.
This is a library, not an application template. No dependencies were installed.
Use the consuming module's pinned matching gofastr-native version.

```go
import (
    "github.com/DonaldMurillo/gofastr-native/device/files"
    "github.com/DonaldMurillo/gofastr-native/extension"
    "github.com/DonaldMurillo/gofastr-native/nativeui"
    documents "your/module/path/to/this/package"
)

screens, err := nativeui.NewWithExtensions("/", []extension.Definition{
    documents.Definition(),
}, routes...)
operation, err := documents.Acquire(files.Pick{
    Title: "Import text", Extensions: []string{".txt"}, MaxBytes: 1024,
})
```

Check both errors. `routes` are the consuming app's ordinary Go routes.
Put `operation` in `nativeui.FileRequest.Extension` and use `screens` as its app.
Handle the file result in Go, checking application authorization and accepting
only the validated document. Consult the consuming app for its route/action setup.

`init` calls `extension/webassets.Register(Definition())` to publish static
compiled assets before GoFastr seals the behavior manifest. Do not move it to
app startup. Publication does NOT select the definition for every app, activate
a host, request permission, or authorize a result; selection remains explicit.

UIKit delegates to the shared single-document foreground task. WebView uses
an explicit browser chooser (`fallback`, not native acquisition). Both enforce
the requested byte bound; Go's `files.Pick.Accept` is authoritative. The scaffold
does not claim camera access or media normalization. Adapt policies and adapters
together; never accept paths, arbitrary URLs, HTML or executable app scripts.

From the module root:

```sh
go test ./path/to/this/package
gofastr-native extension check --pkg ./path/to/this/package
gofastr-native extension check --pkg ./path/to/consuming/bind/package
```

Then build/test the consuming UIKit and WebView apps. Static check validates
manifests/assets/collisions, not Swift/JS behavior or hardware permission UX.
If combining libraries, retain unique names, capability owners and Swift factories.

## Maintainer notes

Keep the JSON manifest, embedded sources, static factory identity/capabilities
and authoritative Go validators in sync. Never replace bounded result validation
with a filename filter. Next time, test cancellation and rejected/oversized
provider results through both renderers before broadening the policy.
